# Migrating Flat Pulumi Program to ComponentResource with Aliases

## The Core Problem

When you move a resource under a `ComponentResource`, its **URN changes** because the URN encodes the parent hierarchy and type. Pulumi identifies resources by URN, so a changed URN looks like *"delete old resource, create new one"* → **replacement**.

`aliases` tell Pulumi *"this new URN is the same resource as that old URN"* so state is re-linked instead of replaced.

---

## Before: Flat Program

```typescript
// index.ts (flat)
import * as aws from "@pulumi/aws";
import * as pulumi from "@pulumi/pulumi";

const config = new pulumi.Config();
const env = pulumi.getStack(); // "dev" or "prod"

const bucket = new aws.s3.BucketV2("app-assets", {
    bucket: `app-assets-${env}`,
});

const versioning = new aws.s3.BucketVersioningV2("app-assets-versioning", {
    bucket: bucket.id,
    versioningConfiguration: { status: "Enabled" },
});

const sse = new aws.s3.BucketServerSideEncryptionConfigurationV2("app-assets-sse", {
    bucket: bucket.id,
    rules: [{ applyServerSideEncryptionByDefault: { sseAlgorithm: "AES256" } }],
});

const pab = new aws.s3.BucketPublicAccessBlock("app-assets-pab", {
    bucket: bucket.id,
    blockPublicAcls: true,
    blockPublicPolicy: true,
    ignorePublicAcls: true,
    restrictPublicBuckets: true,
});
```

At the root, these resources have URNs like:

```
urn:pulumi:dev::proj::aws:s3/bucketV2:BucketV2::app-assets
```

---

## After: ComponentResource

```typescript
// components/AppBucket.ts
import * as aws from "@pulumi/aws";
import * as pulumi from "@pulumi/pulumi";

export interface AppBucketArgs {
    env: string;
    replicationRegion?: string; // prod: "us-west-2"
}

export class AppBucket extends pulumi.ComponentResource {
    public readonly bucket: aws.s3.BucketV2;

    constructor(name: string, args: AppBucketArgs, opts?: pulumi.ComponentResourceOptions) {
        super("myorg:storage:AppBucket", name, {}, opts);

        // Now that parent = this component, each child's URN changes.
        // Alias each child back to its OLD (root-parented) URN.

        this.bucket = new aws.s3.BucketV2(`${name}`, {
            bucket: `app-assets-${args.env}`,
        }, {
            parent: this,
            // OLD urn had no parent (root). Alias by NAME with no parent.
            aliases: [{ name: "app-assets", parent: pulumi.rootStackResource }],
        });

        new aws.s3.BucketVersioningV2(`${name}-versioning`, {
            bucket: this.bucket.id,
            versioningConfiguration: { status: "Enabled" },
        }, {
            parent: this,
            aliases: [{ name: "app-assets-versioning", parent: pulumi.rootStackResource }],
        });

        new aws.s3.BucketServerSideEncryptionConfigurationV2(`${name}-sse`, {
            bucket: this.bucket.id,
            rules: [{ applyServerSideEncryptionByDefault: { sseAlgorithm: "AES256" } }],
        }, {
            parent: this,
            aliases: [{ name: "app-assets-sse", parent: pulumi.rootStackResource }],
        });

        new aws.s3.BucketPublicAccessBlock(`${name}-pab`, {
            bucket: this.bucket.id,
            blockPublicAcls: true,
            blockPublicPolicy: true,
            ignorePublicAcls: true,
            restrictPublicBuckets: true,
        }, {
            parent: this,
            aliases: [{ name: "app-assets-pab", parent: pulumi.rootStackResource }],
        });

        this.registerOutputs({ bucketArn: this.bucket.arn });
    }
}
```

```typescript
// index.ts (new)
import * as pulumi from "@pulumi/pulumi";
import { AppBucket } from "./components/AppBucket";

const env = pulumi.getStack();
const cfg = new pulumi.Config();

// NOTE: the component's logical name must match what the aliases reference.
// If children were named "app-assets-*", keeping name="app-assets" makes
// child names "app-assets-versioning" etc. → matches old flat names.
const appBucket = new AppBucket("app-assets", {
    env,
    replicationRegion: env === "prod" ? "us-west-2" : undefined,
});

export const bucketArn = appBucket.bucket.arn;
```

---

## Key Rules for Alias-Based Migration

### 1. Alias the change, not the identity
The alias describes the resource's **old address**. When wrapping in a component, the two things that changed are:
- **parent** (was root/stack → now the component)
- Sometimes the **type** (only if you renamed the class of resource — not the case here)

So alias `{ name: "<old-name>", parent: pulumi.rootStackResource }`.

### 2. Keep child names identical
The safest migration keeps the resource *name* strings the same. If the old resource was `"app-assets-versioning"`, name the new child so it resolves to the same string. Using template `${name}-versioning` with component name `"app-assets"` reproduces `"app-assets-versioning"`.

### 3. The component itself needs no alias
The `ComponentResource` is new state (it has no physical cloud resource), so it's fine for it to appear as an addition. Only the **leaf cloud resources** need aliases.

### 4. `parent: pulumi.rootStackResource`
This is the modern way to reference the implicit root/stack parent that flat resources had. On older SDKs use the stack's URN directly, or `aliases: [{ name: "old-name" }]` with `parent` omitted — but being explicit avoids ambiguity.

---

## Verification Workflow

```bash
# 1. Preview BEFORE applying — this is the critical safety gate
pulumi preview --diff

# Expect to see:
#   + myorg:storage:AppBucket  app-assets   create   (the component wrapper - fine)
#   ~ aws:s3/bucketV2:BucketV2 app-assets   (no changes / update-in-place)
#
# You must NOT see:
#   +- aws:s3/bucketV2:BucketV2 app-assets   replace
#   -  aws:s3/bucketV2:BucketV2 app-assets   delete

# 2. If any resource shows "replace" or "delete", STOP.
#    An alias is wrong. Check name/parent match.

# 3. Apply per environment independently (isolation criterion #7)
pulumi stack select dev  && pulumi up
pulumi stack select prod && pulumi up
```

If preview shows `replace` on the RDS or bucket, that would violate acceptance criteria (data loss + deletion protection). The whole point of aliases is to reduce that diff to a no-op re-parenting.

---

## Applying the Pattern to the Full Spine

Wrap each logical unit in its own component, aliasing every leaf:

| Component | Wraps | Alias each leaf → root parent |
|---|---|---|
| `AppBucket` | BucketV2, Versioning, SSE, PAB, (prod) Replication | ✅ |
| `Database` | rds.Instance, SubnetGroup, SecurityGroup | ✅ |
| `Cluster` | eks.Cluster, ManagedNodeGroup | ✅ |
| `ServiceAccount` | iam.User/Role, Policy, OIDC provider | ✅ |
| `HttpsFrontend` | CloudFront, ACM cert, listener | ✅ |

Environment differences stay in `Pulumi.dev.yaml` / `Pulumi.prod.yaml` and are read via `pulumi.Config` — the component signatures take an `env`/sizing arg, preserving stack-config-based isolation.

---

## Once Migration Is Confirmed

After a successful `pulumi up` where state is re-linked, the aliases have done their job — state URNs are now the component URNs. You *can* remove the alias arrays in a later cleanup commit. Keep them for at least one release cycle in case of rollback, then delete.

